7499434	t	{"PubmedArticle": {"PubmedData": {"History": {"PubMedPubDate": [{"Day": "1", "Year": "1995", "Month": "12", "@attributes": {"PubStatus": "pubmed"}}, {"Day": "1", "Hour": "0", "Year": "1995", "Month": "12", "Minute": "1", "@attributes": {"PubStatus": "medline"}}, {"Day": "1", "Hour": "0", "Year": "1995", "Month": "12", "Minute": "0", "@attributes": {"PubStatus": "entrez"}}]}, "ArticleIdList": {"ArticleId": ["7499434", "10.1074/jbc.270.48.29012"]}, "PublicationStatus": "ppublish"}, "MedlineCitation": {"PMID": "7499434", "Article": {"Journal": {"ISSN": "0021-9258", "Title": "The Journal of biological chemistry", "JournalIssue": {"Issue": "48", "Volume": "270", "PubDate": {"Day": "01", "Year": "1995", "Month": "Dec"}, "@attributes": {"CitedMedium": "Print"}}, "ISOAbbreviation": "J. Biol. Chem."}, "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "The small splice variant of tenascin-C (TN) has eight fibronectin type III (FN3) domains. The major large splice variant has three (in chicken) or seven (in human) additional FN3 domains inserted between domains five and six. Chiquet-Ehrismann et al. (Chiquet-Ehrismann, R., Matsuoka, Y., Hofer, U., Spring, J., Bernasconi, C., and Chiquet, M. (1991, Cell Regul. 2, 927-938) demonstrated that the small variant bound preferentially to fibronectin in enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay, and only the small variant was incorporated into the matrix by cultures of chicken fibroblasts. Here we have studied human TN, and confirmed that the small variant binds preferentially to purified fibronectin and to fibronectin-containing extracellular matrix. Thus this differential binding appears to be conserved across vertebrate species. Using bacterial expression proteins, we mapped the major binding site to the third FN3 domain of TN. Consistent with this mapping, a monoclonal antibody against an epitope in this domain did not stain TN segments bound to cell culture matrix fibrils. The enhanced binding of the small TN variant suggests the existence of another, weak binding site probably in FN3 domains 6-8, which is only positioned to bind fibronectin in the small splice variant. This binding of domains 6-8 may involve a third molecule present in matrix fibrils, as the enhanced binding of small TN was much more prominent to matrix fibrils than to purified fibronectin."}, "Language": "eng", "GrantList": {"Grant": {"Agency": "NCI NIH HHS", "Acronym": "CA", "Country": "United States", "GrantID": "R37 CA47056"}, "@attributes": {"CompleteYN": "Y"}}, "AuthorList": {"Author": [{"ForeName": "C Y", "Initials": "CY", "LastName": "Chung", "@attributes": {"ValidYN": "Y"}, "AffiliationInfo": {"Affiliation": "Department of Cell Biology, Duke University Medical Center, Durham, North Carolina 27710, USA."}}, {"ForeName": "L", "Initials": "L", "LastName": "Zardi", "@attributes": {"ValidYN": "Y"}}, {"ForeName": "H P", "Initials": "HP", "LastName": "Erickson", "@attributes": {"ValidYN": "Y"}}], "@attributes": {"CompleteYN": "Y"}}, "Pagination": {"MedlinePgn": "29012-7"}, "@attributes": {"PubModel": "Print"}, "ArticleTitle": "Binding of tenascin-C to soluble fibronectin and matrix fibrils.", "PublicationTypeList": {"PublicationType": ["Journal Article", "Research Support, U.S. Gov't, P.H.S."]}}, "@attributes": {"Owner": "NLM", "Status": "MEDLINE"}, "DateRevised": {"Day": "08", "Year": "2019", "Month": "05"}, "ChemicalList": {"Chemical": [{"RegistryNumber": "0", "NameOfSubstance": "Fibronectins"}, {"RegistryNumber": "0", "NameOfSubstance": "Solutions"}, {"RegistryNumber": "0", "NameOfSubstance": "Tenascin"}]}, "DateCompleted": {"Day": "18", "Year": "1996", "Month": "01"}, "CitationSubset": "IM", "MeshHeadingList": {"MeshHeading": [{"DescriptorName": "3T3 Cells"}, {"DescriptorName": "Animals"}, {"DescriptorName": "Cells, Cultured"}, {"DescriptorName": "Chickens"}, {"DescriptorName": "Cricetinae"}, {"QualifierName": "metabolism", "DescriptorName": "Extracellular Matrix"}, {"QualifierName": "metabolism", "DescriptorName": "Fibronectins"}, {"QualifierName": "pathology", "DescriptorName": "Glioma"}, {"DescriptorName": "Humans"}, {"DescriptorName": "Mice"}, {"DescriptorName": "Protein Binding"}, {"DescriptorName": "RNA Splicing"}, {"DescriptorName": "Solubility"}, {"DescriptorName": "Solutions"}, {"QualifierName": ["genetics", "metabolism"], "DescriptorName": "Tenascin"}, {"DescriptorName": "Tumor Cells, Cultured"}]}, "MedlineJournalInfo": {"Country": "United States", "MedlineTA": "J Biol Chem", "ISSNLinking": "0021-9258", "NlmUniqueID": "2985121R"}}}}
8626429	t	{"PubmedArticle": {"PubmedData": {"History": {"PubMedPubDate": [{"Day": "15", "Year": "1996", "Month": "3", "@attributes": {"PubStatus": "pubmed"}}, {"Day": "15", "Hour": "0", "Year": "1996", "Month": "3", "Minute": "1", "@attributes": {"PubStatus": "medline"}}, {"Day": "15", "Hour": "0", "Year": "1996", "Month": "3", "Minute": "0", "@attributes": {"PubStatus": "entrez"}}]}, "ArticleIdList": {"ArticleId": ["8626429", "10.1074/jbc.271.11.6333"]}, "PublicationStatus": "ppublish"}, "MedlineCitation": {"PMID": "8626429", "Article": {"Journal": {"ISSN": "0021-9258", "Title": "The Journal of biological chemistry", "JournalIssue": {"Issue": "11", "Volume": "271", "PubDate": {"Day": "15", "Year": "1996", "Month": "Mar"}, "@attributes": {"CitedMedium": "Print"}}, "ISOAbbreviation": "J. Biol. Chem."}, "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "The nef gene is unique to the primate lentiviruses and encodes a cytoplasmic membrane-associated protein that affects T-cell signaling and is essential for both maintenance of a high virus load in vivo and for disease progression. Here we investigated the perturbation of cell signaling by Nef in T-cells and found that Nef interacts with the T-cell restricted Lek tyrosine kinase both in vitro and in vivo. The molecular basis for this interaction was analyzed. We show that cell-derived Nef is precipitated in a synergistic manner by the recombinant Src homology 2 (SH2) and SH3 domains from Lck. A functional proline-rich motif and the tyrosine phosphorylation of Nef were evidenced as likely participants in this interaction. The precipitation of Nef by the Lck recombinant proteins was specific, since neither Fyn, Csk, p85 phosphatidylinositol 3-kinase nor phospholipase Cgamma SH2 domains coprecipitated Nef from T-cells. Finally, depressed Lck kinase activity resulted from the presence of Nef, both in vitro and in intact cells, and nef expression resulted in impairment of both proximal and distal Lck-mediated signaling events. These results provide a molecular basis for the Nef-induced T-cell signaling defect and its role in AIDS pathogenesis."}, "Language": "eng", "AuthorList": {"Author": [{"ForeName": "Y", "Initials": "Y", "LastName": "Collette", "@attributes": {"ValidYN": "Y"}, "AffiliationInfo": {"Affiliation": "INSERM U119, Marseille, France."}}, {"ForeName": "H", "Initials": "H", "LastName": "Dutartre", "@attributes": {"ValidYN": "Y"}}, {"ForeName": "A", "Initials": "A", "LastName": "Benziane", "@attributes": {"ValidYN": "Y"}}, {"LastName": "Ramos-Morales", "@attributes": {"ValidYN": "Y"}}, {"ForeName": "R", "Initials": "R", "LastName": "Benarous", "@attributes": {"ValidYN": "Y"}}, {"ForeName": "M", "Initials": "M", "LastName": "Harris", "@attributes": {"ValidYN": "Y"}}, {"ForeName": "D", "Initials": "D", "LastName": "Olive", "@attributes": {"ValidYN": "Y"}}], "@attributes": {"CompleteYN": "Y"}}, "Pagination": {"MedlinePgn": "6333-41"}, "@attributes": {"PubModel": "Print"}, "ArticleTitle": "Physical and functional interaction of Nef with Lck. HIV-1 Nef-induced T-cell signaling defects.", "PublicationTypeList": {"PublicationType": ["Journal Article", "Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't"]}}, "@attributes": {"Owner": "NLM", "Status": "MEDLINE"}, "DateRevised": {"Day": "08", "Year": "2019", "Month": "05"}, "ChemicalList": {"Chemical": [{"RegistryNumber": "0", "NameOfSubstance": "Gene Products, nef"}, {"RegistryNumber": "0", "NameOfSubstance": "Recombinant Proteins"}, {"RegistryNumber": "0", "NameOfSubstance": "nef Gene Products, Human Immunodeficiency Virus"}, {"RegistryNumber": "EC 2.7.10.2", "NameOfSubstance": "Lymphocyte Specific Protein Tyrosine Kinase p56(lck)"}, {"RegistryNumber": "EC 2.7.10.2", "NameOfSubstance": "src-Family Kinases"}]}, "DateCompleted": {"Day": "24", "Year": "1996", "Month": "06"}, "CitationSubset": ["IM", "X"], "MeshHeadingList": {"MeshHeading": [{"DescriptorName": "Amino Acid Sequence"}, {"DescriptorName": "Binding Sites"}, {"DescriptorName": "Cell Line"}, {"DescriptorName": "Chemical Precipitation"}, {"QualifierName": ["chemistry", "genetics", "physiology"], "DescriptorName": "Gene Products, nef"}, {"DescriptorName": "Genes, nef"}, {"QualifierName": "etiology", "DescriptorName": "HIV Infections"}, {"QualifierName": ["genetics", "physiology"], "DescriptorName": "HIV-1"}, {"DescriptorName": "Humans"}, {"DescriptorName": "Lymphocyte Specific Protein Tyrosine Kinase p56(lck)"}, {"DescriptorName": "Models, Biological"}, {"DescriptorName": "Molecular Sequence Data"}, {"QualifierName": ["chemistry", "genetics", "metabolism"], "DescriptorName": "Recombinant Proteins"}, {"DescriptorName": "Signal Transduction"}, {"QualifierName": ["physiology", "virology"], "DescriptorName": "T-Lymphocytes"}, {"DescriptorName": "nef Gene Products, Human Immunodeficiency Virus"}, {"QualifierName": "genetics", "DescriptorName": "src Homology Domains"}, {"QualifierName": ["chemistry", "genetics", "physiology"], "DescriptorName": "src-Family Kinases"}]}, "MedlineJournalInfo": {"Country": "United States", "MedlineTA": "J Biol Chem", "ISSNLinking": "0021-9258", "NlmUniqueID": "2985121R"}}}}
